5417] [17592186106739 #edb/ref 1061 623185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106739 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2154c4c2-efe1-5888-87e0-905e612555d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106739 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1061 623186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2896eab9-aca7-5163-8e08-2101c4fa2827" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106740 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1061 623189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ffc5ce2a-4bcb-5660-a1ce-e79bd1b4c604" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106741 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107046 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106742 #edb/ref 1061 623190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106742 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec3bbafa-69db-5676-8013-221157e979a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106742 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105808 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the triangle $\\triangle ABC$ shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46a85010d46969ffb7df440efd72bb3fa8d1394b35cc2887ee4567cda15079c5.png)\n\nThe sum of the length of its sides is called the **perimeter**. Here, the perimeter of our triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+AC \\\\  & =2+4.2+3.8 \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1065 2244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7664d7c3-e47c-57b6-97aa-1131f036852c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107044 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a quadrilateral $ABCD$ as shown below and it is known that ${AD} = 6\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${AB} = 2\\,\\mathrm{m},$ ${BC} = 4\\,\\mathrm{m},$ and $CD=5\\,\\mathrm{m}.$ What is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?   \n\n![](images/a7/a74251f1c72eccad42b98e0d3fd9a7e7cd0a60018a6f03bb65ec2abc34eb1c88.png)\n\nRemember that the **perimeter** $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. So, we can simply calculate \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AD+AB+BC+CD \\\\  & =6+2+4+5 \\\\  & =17\\,m.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1065 2246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0428807f-e6a6-5280-81bc-ab454203aebf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107045 #edb/ref 1067 "Perimeters of Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's take a look at the polygons depicted below and find their perimeters.\n\n![](images/69/69ef06a71f13625ad4525a03966f4a4c62ae2b2136b085e97b8dc02a6a6963cc.png)\n\nFrom the picture, we conclude that polygons I, II and III all have a perimeter of $10$ while polygon IV has a perimeter of $8.$ \n\nPolygons that have the same perimeter are called **isoperimetric**. So I, II and III are isoperimetric polygons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1065 2247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "596db00e-9cf7-51e1-902e-73c6670780eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107046 #edb/ref 1067 "Isoperimetric Polygons" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553843 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186554648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553843 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186554659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553843 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186554667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553843 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553843 #edb/ref 1045 "The Perimeter of a Polygon" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553843 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186554667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553843 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553843 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186553843 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4303ae32-a6ba-5a31-8a6a-e56669